% KISS, reused from föreläsningen Funktioner (bilaga C there).

% === provenance: Rich1995Johnson ===
% CLAIM: The KISS principle -- "Keep it simple, stupid" -- is attributed
%   to Clarence L. (Kelly) Johnson, who led Lockheed's Skunk Works.
% FOUND-VIA: the scholarly providers do not index the origin question
%   (session variables-kiss, queries Q1--Q3 returned only papers *using*
%   KISS in unrelated domains).  Reached instead through
%   WebFetch https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KISS_principle, whose origin
%   sentence cites this memoir; the memoir PDF itself was located with
%   WebSearch 'Ben Rich "Clarence Leonard" Kelly Johnson 1910-1990
%   Biographical Memoir National Academy of Sciences PDF' and downloaded
%   from the mirror http://biographicalmemoirs.org/pdfs/johnson-clarence.pdf
%   (the nasonline.org copy returns HTTP 403 to non-browser clients).
% PICKED: the best-placed source that exists.  Written by Ben R. Rich,
%   Johnson's own successor as head of the Skunk Works, and published by
%   the National Academy of Sciences, so it is a reputable secondary
%   source by someone with first-hand knowledge.  It is also the source
%   that Wikipedia's origin claim rests on, so citing it cites the
%   actual evidence rather than the encyclopaedia.  Preferred over
%   Johnson's own autobiography (Kelly: More Than My Share of It All,
%   1985), which is lending-restricted on archive.org and whose text
%   could not be searched -- see the report's closed-access list.
% QUOTE (p. 231): Rich quotes Johnson on how the Skunk Works worked:
%   "Our aim," he said, "is to get results cheaper, sooner, and better
%   through application of common sense to tough problems.  If it works,
%   don't fix it."  "Reduce reports and other paper work to a minimum."
%   "Keep it simple, stupid---KISS---is our constant reminder."
% VERIFIED: full-text PDF read (pdftotext; the quoted passage sits on
%   printed p. 231, PDF p. 13 of 24 -- note that Wikipedia's "p. 13"
%   cites the PDF page, not the printed page).  IMPORTANT SCOPE LIMIT:
%   the passage establishes that Johnson USED KISS as a Skunk Works
%   motto.  It does NOT say he coined the phrase, gives no date, and is
%   a recollection published five years after Johnson's death.  The
%   deck must therefore say "attributed to", not "coined by".
% COUNTER: WebFetch of Lockheed Martin's own page
%   https://www.lockheedmartin.com/en-us/news/features/history/johnson.html
%   says only "His favorite maxim? KISS---Keep It Simple, Stupid.",
%   again usage and not coinage; and the company's own "Kelly's 14 Rules
%   and Practices"
%   (https://www.lockheedmartin.com/content/dam/lockheed-martin/aero/photo/skunkworks/kellys-14-rules.pdf,
%   read in full) does not contain KISS at all.  Decisive counter-
%   evidence against coinage is in [[Popik2019KISS]]: the phrase is in
%   print from 1958 in a civilian office-signs story with no Lockheed
%   connection.  Claim retained only in the weak "attributed to" form.
% FOUND-VIA (here): backed in föreläsningen Funktioner (modules/variables/
%   slides-functions, bilaga B/C); entry and provenance copied verbatim.
% DATE: 2026-09-03
@incollection{Rich1995Johnson,
  author    = {Rich, Ben R.},
  title     = {Clarence Leonard (Kelly) Johnson 1910--1990: A Biographical Memoir},
  booktitle = {Biographical Memoirs},
  publisher = {National Academies Press},
  address   = {Washington, DC},
  year      = {1995},
  pages     = {221--241}
}

% === provenance: Popik2019KISS ===
% CLAIM: The earliest documented uses of "Keep It Simple, Stupid" are
%   from 1958--1961 and are civilian and US Navy, not Lockheed; the
%   Kelly Johnson coinage story is not supported by dated evidence.
% FOUND-VIA: WebSearch '"keep it simple stupid" origin Kelly Johnson
%   Lockheed earliest documented use' and WebSearch 'greensdictofslang
%   "KISS" "keep it simple stupid" entry citations 1960', which both
%   surfaced barrypopik.com; page then retrieved with curl and its
%   citation list read verbatim
%   (https://barrypopik.com/blog/kiss_keep_it_simple_silly).
% PICKED: Barry Popik is a professional lexicographer and antedater
%   whose newspaper citations are given with paper, date, page and
%   column, so each one is independently checkable.  Preferred over the
%   Wikipedia article (which is a tertiary summary) and over
%   [[GreenKISS]], whose earliest quotation is 13 years later.  Dalzell's
%   Routledge Dictionary of Modern American Slang (2009, p. 595), which
%   Wikipedia cites for the 1960 Navy item, could not be opened and is
%   therefore not cited here -- Popik quotes the same underlying
%   newspaper item directly.
% QUOTE: the earliest citation, Rocky Mount (NC) Sunday Telegram,
%   4 May 1958, "Feet on the Desk" by Pete Ivey, p. 1B col. 7:
%   "CHAPEL HILL---Signs with the single word, KISS, are being tacked up
%   on walls in offices and other places of business [...] he asked the
%   boss point blank, 'What do the four letters, K-I-S-S mean?'  'Keep It
%   Simple, Stupid,' replied the boy."  Popik's own gloss: "The first
%   'KISS' citation, however, appears in print in the Rocky Mount (NC)
%   Sunday Telegram on May 4, 1958, and does not appear to be related to
%   the military."  Further dated citations he gives: New York Times,
%   22 Oct 1959, p. 42 ("I use the KISS technique---that is, Keep it
%   Simple, Stupid."); Chicago Daily Tribune, 4 Dec 1960, p. 43 ("Rear
%   Adm. Paul D. Stroop [...] has instituted 'Project KISS' [...] KISS
%   stands for 'Keep it simple, stupid.'"); Salt Lake Tribune,
%   13 Feb 1961, p. 19; Washington Post, 8 Nov 1964, p. C3.
% VERIFIED: page fetched with curl, HTML stripped, and the citation
%   list read in full; quotes above are transcribed from that text.
%   The underlying newspaper pages themselves were NOT inspected --
%   Popik cites them from Newspapers.com, which is paywalled.  This is a
%   secondary transcription and is flagged as such in the report.
% COUNTER: the support-side search for a documented Lockheed origin was
%   run in both directions and came back empty: WebSearch 'Kelly Johnson
%   "More Than My Share of It All" 1985 autobiography "keep it simple
%   stupid" KISS page' and WebSearch 'Ben Rich "Skunk Works" 1994 book
%   "keep it simple stupid" Kelly Johnson quote' produced no dated
%   first-hand statement, only blog and consultancy pages repeating the
%   attribution without evidence.  No source was found that dates a
%   Johnson use of KISS earlier than 1958.
% FOUND-VIA (here): backed in föreläsningen Funktioner (modules/variables/
%   slides-functions, bilaga B/C); entry and provenance copied verbatim.
% DATE: 2026-09-03
@misc{Popik2019KISS,
  author       = {Popik, Barry},
  title        = {K.I.S.S. (Keep It Simple, Silly)},
  howpublished = {The Big Apple, \url{https://barrypopik.com/blog/kiss_keep_it_simple_silly}},
